<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-4-2-effects">
  <time minutes="90" />
  <title>Effects of Technology</title>

  <idx>effects-of-technology</idx>
  <introduction>
    <p>Quick Links:
      <xref text="global" ref="beneficial-and-harmful-effects, impacts-beyond-intended-purpose,
      LOs-4-2, vocab-4-2"/>
    </p>
    <p>
      In this topic, we analyze how technology can create both benefits and harms,
      including effects beyond intended use.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="beneficial-and-harmful-effects">
    <title>Beneficial and Harmful Effects</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.2.A.1</title>
      <p>
        Technology is created and used by people, and human choices shape how it affects others.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.2.A.2</title>
      <p>
        When a new technology is adopted, it often changes how people complete tasks by making
        some actions easier, faster, or more convenient, which sometimes changes habits and expectations.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.2.A.3</title>
      <p>
        A new technology can have both beneficial and harmful effects depending on the user,
        the situation, and what the person values. The same person may view an effect as
        beneficial in one context and harmful in another.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.2.A.4</title>
      <p>
        Advances in technology can increase creativity and capabilities in many fields
        (e.g., medicine, engineering, communication, and the arts). They can also create
        risks when tools are misused to cause harm, when people over-rely on outputs
        without checking accuracy, when misinformation spreads faster or seems more believable,
        or when unequal access limits opportunities and widens gaps in education, jobs,
        or services.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="impacts-beyond-intended-purpose">
    <title>Impacts Beyond Intended Purpose</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.2.B.1</title>
      <p>
        Digital tools are often used in ways their creators did not intend, especially when users
        adapt features for new goals or when the tool spreads to new contexts.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.2.B.2</title>
      <p>
        Technologies can have impacts beyond their intended use because they can change people's
        behavior, what information is shared, and who has access to opportunities or influence.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.2.B.3</title>
      <p>
        As technologies spread, they can also create unequal impacts. The digital divide refers
        to differences in access to computing devices and the Internet based on socioeconomic,
        geographic, or demographic factors.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.2.B.4</title>
      <p>
        The digital divide affects both groups and individuals by shaping who can participate
        fully in digital spaces and benefit from technology. It can be affected by the actions
        of individuals, organizations, and governments.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.2.B.5</title>
      <p>
        Because impacts can be uneven, responsible developers consider who may be unable to
        access or use the technology and who may be negatively affected by its design or use.
        They design features and supports to reduce barriers to access, usability, and participation.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.2.B.6</title>
      <p>
        Developers cannot predict every future use of a tool or system, especially once it is
        widely shared. However, they can anticipate likely risks, test with real users, and update
        the system over time as new impacts appear.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.2.B.7</title>
      <p>
        Technology tools can have effects beyond their main purpose. When many people use a tool,
        its features can reshape habits, relationships, and opportunities in ways that were not
        planned or predicted. Recognizing these broader effects helps people make more informed
        choices about how they use a tool and helps developers revise designs more responsibly.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="LOs-4-2">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>

    <remark>
      <title>4.2.A</title>
      <p>
        Explain how effects of technology can be both beneficial and harmful.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>4.2.B</title>
      <p>
        Explain how technology can have an impact beyond its intended purpose.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="vocab-4-2">
   <title>Vocabulary Review</title>
  </subsection>
</section>
